    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p5-q48-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Write down the elements in the ‘second’ column $(k=2)$ of Pascal’s triangle. What do you notice about these values in relation to the triangular numbers?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1,3,6,10,15,\\ldots$; these are $t_1,t_2,t_3,t_4,t_5,\\ldots$.",
          "worked": "Reading down column $k=2$, starting at row $n=2$, gives $1,3,6,10,15,\\ldots$. These are the triangular numbers $t_1,t_2,t_3,t_4,t_5,\\ldots$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q48-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Is this pattern guaranteed to continue? If so, explain why this is the case.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Yes. Pascal’s addition rule gives $t_n=t_{n-1}+n$, the same recurrence as the triangular dot pattern.",
          "worked": "Yes. The entries in column $k=1$ are $1,2,3,4,\\ldots$. Each new entry in column $k=2$ is the previous entry in that column plus the entry above left in column $k=1$. Hence\n$$t_n=t_{n-1}+n\\qquad(n\\ge2).$$\nThe triangular dot pattern also adds $n$ dots at stage $n$. Both sequences start at $t_1=1$, so they continue to agree.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q48-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What can be said about the entries of the $k=3$ column of Pascal’s triangle and the triangular numbers?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "They are the running totals of the triangular numbers: $1,4,10,20,35,\\ldots$.",
          "worked": "Each entry in column $k=3$ is the previous entry in that column plus the triangular number above left in column $k=2$. Starting at row $n=3$, the entries are therefore\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=t_1\\\\4&=t_1+t_2\\\\10&=t_1+t_2+t_3\\\\20&=t_1+t_2+t_3+t_4\\end{align*}$$\nThus column $k=3$ contains the running totals of the triangular numbers.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
